WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:01.660
Carter Thallon: On this episode

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:03.751
of "Pop Culture Decoded Daily,"

00:00:03.751 --> 00:00:05.670
we explain why we think
"Chernobyl" won the Emmy

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.430
for best cinematography.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:09.580
We break down why the
new "Rambo" won't be able

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.230
to live up to the original.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.490
And, did you notice any Easter eggs

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.774
in the new "Birds of Prey" poster?

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:26.050
Nate Lee: Hi, welcome to
"Pop Culture Decoded Daily."

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:29.200
We are producers and
filmmakers here at Insider.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.140
And we're gonna break
apart some of the movies

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.370
and TV series that you guys love.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:35.490
Carter: So, each episode,
Nate and I are going

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.120
to take a look at a clip
that's relevant in the news,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.270
that's been trending, people
have been talking about,

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.240
and get into what each of us
really loved about that piece

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.770
and some of the technical
aspects that went into it

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.550
that you might not have noticed at first.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:49.383
Nate: Sounds good.

00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:50.670
So, what are we talking about this week?

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.340
Carter: So, the clip
that I brought in today

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:54.431
is from "Chernobyl."

00:00:54.431 --> 00:00:57.122
"Chernobyl," nominated for 19 Emmy awards.

00:00:57.122 --> 00:00:58.830
Already won a couple Emmy awards

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.010
in the Creative Arts Emmys,

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:01.870
which happened this past weekend.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:05.000
Those are for the more
technical side of the Emmys.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.875
It won for cinematography.

00:01:06.875 --> 00:01:08.258
Which is what I want to talk about.

00:01:08.258 --> 00:01:10.800
Nate: So, which episode are we
talking about, specifically?

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.366
Carter: So, we are talking about

00:01:12.366 --> 00:01:14.682
the second episode of the series,

00:01:14.682 --> 00:01:16.550
and it's at the very end of the episode,

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.367
the reactor's already exploded,

00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:21.930
three men are going into the
reactor to go turn on a valve

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.550
to prevent another
explosion from happening,

00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:27.430
and they're exposing themselves
to a bunch of radiation,

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.140
essentially a suicide mission.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:30.085
So, let's take a look.

00:01:30.085 --> 00:01:31.089
Nate: OK.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:36.269
[Geiger counters clicking]

00:01:36.269 --> 00:01:37.102
Look.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.383
Carter: Usually with cinematography,

00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:42.157
people think of often,
like, "Lawrence of Arabia,"

00:01:42.157 --> 00:01:45.060
like, big, bright, beautiful landscapes.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.520
And one of the reasons I
brought it in is 'cause

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:50.080
you don't really think of
darkness as cinematography.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.490
Cinematographers have a lot of jobs,

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:53.690
but one of their main
jobs, and most important,

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:55.350
is lighting a scene.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.500
Filming for dark scenes is very difficult.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.809
You need to balance it
between it being too bright

00:02:00.809 --> 00:02:02.318
and too dark where you can't see anything.

00:02:02.318 --> 00:02:03.151
Nate: Right, to still
be able to see things.

00:02:03.151 --> 00:02:06.510
Carter: Yeah, exactly, so, for example,

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.868
right here, when his light is going out,

00:02:09.868 --> 00:02:13.320
as you can see, this is
the only light that should

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.300
be illuminating the scene,
but in the background here.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.520
Nate: Yeah, I see it.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:19.440
Carter: There's some lighting.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.945
And, again, up here with
the top of the pipe there.

00:02:22.945 --> 00:02:24.230
Nate: Yeah, where does
that light come from?

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.063
Carter: Yeah, where's
that light come from?

00:02:25.063 --> 00:02:25.896
Nate: Right, right.

00:02:25.896 --> 00:02:28.490
Carter: So, that's one of
the important considerations

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.390
that cinematographers
need to take into account

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.890
is how much lighting do I need to put in

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.873
where it's realistic,

00:02:36.873 --> 00:02:39.610
but also enough lighting where I can see.

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:41.080
So, I can actually show some

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.300
of the behind-the-scenes photos.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.440
Here's what the set looks like.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.165
As you can see here,

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:49.090
there are two big overhead
lights with diffusers on them

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:52.493
to make the entire scene
a little bit brighter.

00:02:52.493 --> 00:02:56.630
It's always gonna be easier
to make a scene darker

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:59.470
in post-production than
to lighten it back up.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:02.370
Nate: Ah, so, you know, something that

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.645
is reminding me a lot of

00:03:03.645 --> 00:03:04.880
is "Game of Thrones," you know,

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.644
there was that huge controversy about...

00:03:06.644 --> 00:03:07.477
Carter: Yeah.

00:03:07.477 --> 00:03:08.700
Nate: People saying,
"I can't see anything,"

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.420
especially that "Battle
of Winterfell" episode,

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:12.870
where it was just pitch darkness.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.850
No one had any idea what was going on,

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.260
but, you know, this...not to throw shade,

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:20.880
but this seems to be a great example

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.830
that shows, like, you
can still convey darkness

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.550
without actually being dark,

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:26.380
and still being able to see things.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:27.440
Carter: Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.450
And I do think they were
at a bit of a disadvantage

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:30.860
with a bit more actiony.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:31.774
Nate: Right, right, of course.

00:03:31.774 --> 00:03:33.340
Carter: But it was pretty dark.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.270
Nate: Right, I mean, their
sort of explanation was

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.360
that they were trying to use
the lighting from that era,

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.470
which was, like, candles and fire.

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:40.303
Carter: True.

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:42.440
Nate: But, I don't know, I
just couldn't see anything.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:43.273
It was really frustrating.

00:03:43.273 --> 00:03:44.170
Carter: Yeah, yeah.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:45.890
So, anyway, what clip do
you wanna talk about today?

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:48.740
Nate: Right, so, as you
know, "Rambo: Last Blood"

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:49.940
is coming to theaters.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.400
A lot of people aren't
interested, I think.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.461
Starting to dislike you.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.290
Nate: Personally, I'm a
big fan of the original,

00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:59.720
so I gotta talk about it.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:02.166
So, I don't really wanna talk about

00:04:02.166 --> 00:04:03.591
the technical aspect of it,

00:04:03.591 --> 00:04:05.839
I wanna talk more about the film itself.

00:04:05.839 --> 00:04:06.672
Carter: Yeah.

00:04:06.672 --> 00:04:08.350
Nate: 'Cause, you know,
it is such a classic.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:11.000
It is, like, a legendary-status movie,

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.030
so, you know, like, sort of, why is that?

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:17.032
So, here's sort of where I
wanna talk about "Rambo,"

00:04:17.032 --> 00:04:20.310
is, you know, if you've
seen the first one,

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.210
it was a very message-driven movie.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:24.043
Carter: Yeah, I feel like that.

00:04:24.043 --> 00:04:28.152
Nate: Right, it was about,
essentially it was about PTSD.

00:04:28.152 --> 00:04:32.500
It showed Rambo as sort of
like this really hurt hero

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.650
who was still a hero, but
he was very hurt from, like,

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.975
the war and the memories
of, like, genocide

00:04:37.975 --> 00:04:40.360
and everything like that.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.730
And all of a sudden,
what's the first image

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.220
that comes to your mind
when I say "Rambo"?

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.180
It is the mass killing, right!

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.020
Where he's like, has a huge
machine gun and he's firing.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.110
It's like, I think, most people.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:53.365
Carter: Most people, yeah.

00:04:53.365 --> 00:04:55.673
Nate: But that's, you
know, that's actually not

00:04:55.673 --> 00:04:57.500
sort of the image from "Rambo I,"

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.580
that's actually, like, more "Rambo II."

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.413
Carter: Yeah.

00:05:00.413 --> 00:05:04.410
Nate: It is really weird, 'cause
"Rambo I" is such a classic

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.430
and was, you know,
loved by critics so much

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:09.870
because it had such a clear message,

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.812
and showing a story that
we've never seen before

00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:14.344
of, like, PTSD.

00:05:14.344 --> 00:05:16.926
So, it carried on that,
and many people think

00:05:16.926 --> 00:05:19.200
sort of that message
disappeared by the second one,

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.090
but that's not true, you know,
if you watch the second one,

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.801
there's still a lot of that, like,

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:25.940
lines about the nihilistic side of war

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:29.350
and how, like, we're all
products of the government

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:30.690
and things like that.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.220
And then, by third.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.053
Carter: Just action.

00:05:33.053 --> 00:05:33.969
Nate: It was gone.
Carter: Yeah.

00:05:33.969 --> 00:05:37.250
Nate: You know, I think it's
also, it changed how action

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.780
was portrayed, 'cause
first one, if you see it,

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.670
Rambo's really, really
tactical, he's always hiding.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:48.413
And then, from second and
third one, he's invincible.

00:05:48.413 --> 00:05:49.246
Carter: Absolutely.

00:05:49.246 --> 00:05:50.270
Nate: Which, yeah, I
think it's mainly because

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:51.960
it changed that message, you know,

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:55.630
it changed from sort
of focusing on the PTSD

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:58.623
and the damages of war to,
like, yeah, action hero,

00:05:58.623 --> 00:06:00.670
like, let's mow some people down.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.803
I think it's very ironic that, you know,

00:06:02.803 --> 00:06:04.961
someone who sort of represented, like,

00:06:04.961 --> 00:06:06.647
the dark side of government
and how we're sending

00:06:06.647 --> 00:06:09.020
these people to wars and not
really taking care of them,

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:11.680
then, after a couple movies,
become, like, an American hero,

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.838
and he's, like, you know,
the quintessential symbolism

00:06:15.838 --> 00:06:19.296
of, like, that era, like, strong America,

00:06:19.296 --> 00:06:20.498
like, we gotta go to other countries

00:06:20.498 --> 00:06:22.560
and, like, protect our
interests kind of a thing?

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:23.393
Carter: Yeah.

00:06:23.393 --> 00:06:25.769
Nate: Which I always found
really interesting and ironic

00:06:25.769 --> 00:06:27.253
at the same time.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:31.240
Carter: So, our team here
at Insider can't wait

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:33.489
for the new "Birds of
Prey" trailer to come out.

00:06:33.489 --> 00:06:34.340
Nate: We need an official one.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:35.990
Carter: Yeah, but in the meantime,

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:38.167
they dropped a poster the other day,

00:06:38.167 --> 00:06:40.680
and we just wanted to point
out a couple Easter eggs

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:42.059
that we were pretty excited about.

00:06:42.059 --> 00:06:44.740
Now, they released an extremely
high-resolution poster.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:45.700
Nate: They knew we were looking.

00:06:45.700 --> 00:06:47.392
Carter: Yeah, they knew that we were

00:06:47.392 --> 00:06:48.749
looking for the Easter eggs.

00:06:48.749 --> 00:06:52.850
So, if you look here, Harley Quinn,

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:56.450
she has crossed out

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:58.224
the references to Puddin,

00:06:58.224 --> 00:07:00.810
which is her pet name
for the Joker, obviously.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.155
Jared Leto's Joker,

00:07:02.155 --> 00:07:05.006
seems like he probably won't be appearing.

00:07:05.006 --> 00:07:08.740
So there's that, and then
another one that we found

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.840
was on Ewan McGregor's character.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:15.980
He plays the Black Mask,
but as you can see here,

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:20.610
he has a RS, a very tiny
RS on his left glove there,

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:24.820
and that's a reference to
his character Roman Sionis,

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.460
who then turns into the Black Mask later.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:30.480
Nate: Well, that's it for this episode

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.470
of "Pop Culture Decoded Daily."

00:07:32.470 --> 00:07:33.893
We will see you next time.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:36.362
Carter: Oh boy.

00:07:36.362 --> 00:07:37.850
[laughing]

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:38.683
Off to a good start.

00:07:38.683 --> 00:07:39.516
Producer: You're doing great.

00:07:39.516 --> 00:07:40.349
Carter: God.

